</code></pre> <p>The outer loop selects each row. The inner loop visits each element in that row.</p>

Encapsulated Attendance<pre><code>class Pupil:

    def __init__(self, name):
        self.name = name
        self.__attendance = 0

    def get_attendance(self):
        return self.__attendance

    def set_attendance(self, attendance):
        if 0 &lt;= attendance &lt;= 100:
            self.__attendance = attendance
        else:
            print("Attendance must be between 0 and 100.")

    def display_details(self):
        print("Name:", self.name)
        print("Attendance:", str(self.__attendance) + "%")


pupil1 = Pupil("Alice")
pupil1.set_attendance(95)
pupil1.display_details()
</code></pre> <p>Output:</p> <pre><code>Name: Alice
Attendance: 95%
</code></pre>

Person, Pupil and Teacher<pre><code>class Person:

    def __init__(self, name):
        self.name = name

    def display_name(self):
        print("Name:", self.name)


class Pupil(Person):

    def __init__(self, name, year_group):
        super().__init__(name)
        self.year_group = year_group


class Teacher(Person):

    def __init__(self, name, subject):
        super().__init__(name)
        self.subject = subject


pupil1 = Pupil("Alice", 6)
teacher1 = Teacher("Mrs Smith", "Computing")

pupil1.display_name()
print("Year group:", pupil1.year_group)

teacher1.display_name()
print("Subject:", teacher1.subject)
</code></pre>

Polymorphic Display Method<pre><code>class Person:

    def __init__(self, name):
        self.name = name

    def display_details(self):
        print("Person:", self.name)


class Pupil(Person):

    def __init__(self, name, year_group):
        super().__init__(name)
        self.year_group = year_group

    def display_details(self):
        print("Pupil:", self.name)
        print("Year group:", self.year_group)


class Teacher(Person):

    def __init__(self, name, subject):
        super().__init__(name)
        self.subject = subject

    def display_details(self):
        print("Teacher:", self.name)
        print("Subject:", self.subject)


people = [
    Pupil("Alice", 6),
    Teacher("Mrs Smith", "Computing")
]

for person in people:
    person.display_details()
    print()
</code></pre>

Python <pre><code>numbers = [3, 7, 12, 18, 23, 29, 34, 41, 50]

def binary_search(array, target):
    low = 0
    high = len(array) - 1

    while low &lt;= high:
        mid = (low + high) // 2

        if array[mid] == target:
            return mid
        elif target &lt; array[mid]:
            high = mid - 1
        else:
            low = mid + 1

    return -1

print(binary_search(numbers, 23))
</code></pre>

